      "title": "Mutual Action Plans (MAPs) & Joint Evaluation Agreements",
      "summary": "Eliminate deal slippage: designing Mutual Action Plans (MAPs), establishing Joint Evaluation Agreements (JEAs), creating mutual accountability, and managing buying committee milestones.",
      "readingTimeMinutes": 40,
      "videoUrl": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F3_6eO8bQ2w",
      "body": "<h2>1. The Problem with Unilateral Sales Timelines</h2>\n<p>Sales representatives constantly construct internal spreadsheets with optimistic closing dates: 'This deal will close by September 30 so I can hit my Q3 quota.' The fundamental flaw is that <strong>the customer does not know or care about your company's fiscal quarter end</strong>. Left to their own devices, enterprise buying committees move at glacial corporate speed: committee reviews get postponed, security questionnaires sit unread for weeks, and procurement pushes renewals into the next fiscal year.</p>\n<p>A <strong>Mutual Action Plan (MAP)</strong>—also termed a Joint Evaluation Agreement (JEA) or Mutual Success Plan—transforms an uncoordinated sales process into a shared, co-authored project plan where both buyer and vendor commit to explicit chronological milestones to achieve a shared business outcome.</p>\n<h2>2. The Psychology of Mutual Accountability</h2>\n<p>A MAP is not a sales contract; it is an executive project management charter. By framing the timeline around the <strong>Customer's Desired Go-Live Date</strong>, the AE shifts the psychological burden from 'help me make a sale' to 'ensure you don't miss your strategic corporate launch deadline':</p>\n<pre><code>Strategic Reframe:\n'Sarah, to ensure your commercial underwriting team can launch automated lending by November 15\n(in time for your peak holiday lending surge), here is the backwards timeline of governance\nand technical milestones we must jointly complete starting this week.'</code></pre>\n<h2>3. Chronological Anatomy of an Enterprise MAP</h2>\n<table class=\"comparison-table\">\n  <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Key Milestone</th><th>Owner (Vendor / Client)</th><th>Target Date</th><th>Exit Evidence / Deliverable</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Phase 1: Discovery</strong></td><td>Validate Executive Problem & COI</td><td>AE + Champion</td><td>Day 7</td><td>Mutually signed Business Case & Value Matrix.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Phase 2: Technical</strong></td><td>Architecture & Security Clearance</td><td>SE + Client CISO / Architect</td><td>Day 21</td><td>SOC 2 Review complete; API architecture sign-off.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Phase 3: Business</strong></td><td>Economic Buyer Alignment Meeting</td><td>AE + Champion + Client EB</td><td>Day 35</td><td>EB confirms budget allocation and business case ROI.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Phase 4: Commercial</strong></td><td>Legal MSA & Pricing Redlines</td><td>Vendor Legal + Client Procurement</td><td>Day 50</td><td>Clean legal redlines; agreed Master Services Agreement.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Phase 5: Execution</strong></td><td>Contract Execution & PO Issuance</td><td>Client Procurement + Vendor Finance</td><td>Day 60</td><td>DocuSign executed; Purchase Order generated.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Phase 6: Onboarding</strong></td><td>Implementation Kickoff & Training</td><td>Customer Success + Client Ops Lead</td><td>Day 75</td><td>Admin training complete; production cutover.</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>4. Securing Champion Buy-In to the MAP</h2>\n<p>Never present a MAP as a completed dictate. Co-author it live during an alignment session: <em>'Let's look at your calendar between now and November 15. Who in your procurement team needs to review this, and how long do legal redlines typically take at your bank? Let's pencil in those buffer windows together.'</em></p>\n<h2>5. Using the MAP to Reschedule Deals Objectively</h2>\n<p>When a client misses a critical milestone (e.g., procurement review delayed by two weeks), the AE uses the MAP to have a transparent, objective conversation: <em>'Sarah, since the InfoSec questionnaire was delayed by 14 days, our backwards timeline shows that target November 15 go-live will push into December unless we compress legal review. Would you like to adjust the launch date, or should we schedule a joint call with legal this Thursday to recover time?'</em></p>"
